connected environment, that allows effective data exchange among el­ ements and actors of the transport system may be more prevalent in the future. The information on surrounding traffic and areas beyond the visual range can be provided to improve drivers’ knowledge of the driving situation and avoid dangerous incidents in the connected envi­ ronment [13]. Meanwhile, augmented reality head-up display (ARHUD) is emerging as the latest generation of in-vehicle HUD display technology [14]. AR refers to the combination of real and artificial el­ ements in the line of sight, with the aim of highlighting important ob­ jects to improve human performance [15]. The integration of AR technology and HUD technology allows the combination of virtual and real information in the driver’s primary field of view, and the reflected information appears to be part of the driving situation itself [16]. In this context, the AR-HUD system is supposed to be an effective in-vehicle display form of connected information. However, the complexity of driving behavior indicates that the design of these systems should be in accordance with the needs and preferences of drivers; otherwise, the system cannot work well and may even negatively affect driving behavior [1]. Even technologies that meet 100 % of their objectives are only effective if they are (a) accepted and (b) used [17]. Therefore, drivers’ acceptance is essential for the implementation of the AR-HUD systems.
Some studies have discussed the subjective evaluation of AR-HUD systems. Scho¨mig et al. (2018) stated that the additional AR-HUD as a supplement to the human machine interface (HMI) designed for auto­ mated driving is highly accepted, and the AR concept was rated to be more understandable and more useful [18]. Braun et al. (2018) indi­ cated that ADAS was well accepted and that most participants perceived them as useful, reassuring, and trustworthy. Among them, usefulness was predicted by the acceptance aspects of reassurance, trust, and annoyance [19]. Smith et al. (2021) found that the methods in which AR-HUD presents the information can impact driver scanning behaviors and preferences [20]. Guo et al. (2014) used stated preference data to obtain the driver’s need for driving information and their preference for information presentation and display zone images [1]. Researchers have also explored the effects that AR graphics have on a driver’s distance estimation/depth perception in an augmented side view mirror, implying that AR graphics may be useful and distracting at the same time [21]. However, drivers seem inclined to trust HUDs and may prefer to use them [19,20], even when the addition of AR cues is confusing [22]. In addition, the reasons for the lack of acceptance were also dis­ cussed. Previous research analyzed the discrepancy between aging drivers’ awareness and willingness to use ADAS and found that the barrier most often mentioned to prevent them from purchasing and using ADAS was a lack of perceived usefulness (32 %), followed by functional limitations, high costs (each 9 %), and trust issues (8 %) [24]. Some research reported that a possible reason for the lack of acceptance was that the focus of HUD design has been on implementing technology rather than accommodating users [1]. HUD may be considered an obstacle for driving [25], and thus increase the accident rate due to the driver’s lack of visual focus [26].
Quantitative studies of drivers’ acceptance of emerging technology in the connected environment such as cooperative vehicle infrastructure systems (CVIS), autonomous vehicles (AVs), and advanced in-vehicle information systems have been conducted. The studies of the above emerging technologies can provide a reference for the research on ARHUD acceptance in connected environments. CVIS is an important component of intelligent transportation systems, which can compre­ hensively achieve the dynamic and real-time V2V and V2I information exchange, expand the space and time boundaries of traffic information, diversify the means for obtaining the information, and widely carry out the active safety control of the vehicle and the cooperative control of the road [27]. Zhang et al.(2021) used the technology acceptance model (TAM) to measure the public acceptance of CVIS and gender differences [28]; Li et al. (2021) established an extended TAM with four extended variables (warning service quality, distraction perception, personal

