innovation, and trust) to analyze drivers’ acceptance and influencing factors of CVIS [29]; AVs are those which are capable of sensing their environments in order to perform at least some aspects of the safe­ ty–critical control (like steering, throttling, or braking) without direct human input [30]. Both connected human-driven vehicles and con­ nected automated vehicles are components of the connected environ­ ment. Xu et al. (2021) found that perceived usefulness, trust, and perceived safety were positive predictors of participants’ intention to use and willingness to take AVs more than once [31]; Roberts et al. (2012) studied drivers’ acceptance of two driving assistance technolo­ gies based on the TAM model with simulated driving [32]; Park et al. (2015) considered four psychological factors of car navigation systems (perceived locational accuracy, satisfaction, perceived system reli­ ability, and service and display quality) based on foundational TAM. They showed that the service & display quality components of the sys­ tems were the most significant determinants of drivers’ attitudes toward and intention to use car navigation systems [33]. Other researchers further examined the influence of drivers’ age, gender, and road envi­ ronment on the acceptance of ADAS through real vehicle experiments [34,35].
In general, the huge potential of AR-HUD is undeniable in the field of connected autonomous driving [36], and drivers’ acceptance of and willingness to use it are key prerequisites [17,37]. It was demonstrated that drivers’ characteristics, system service quality, and psychological perception in the interaction process are potential factors affecting drivers’ acceptance of the emerging system. However, quantitative research on AR-HUD acceptance based on a theoretical model is still immature. It is necessary to clarify the uncertainty of drivers’ accep­ tance of AR-HUD in the connected environment and its influencing factors. To address the above questions, this paper will comprehensively consider drivers’ characteristics, system service quality, and the human–computer interaction process to examine drivers’ acceptance of AR-HUD in connected environments based on TAM theory and explore the effect of potential factors. The study contributes to solving the imbalance between AR-HUD design and users’ needs and realizing the potential advantages in driving safety improvement.
The rest of the article is organized as follows: Section 2 provides the details of the theoretical background and hypotheses. Section 3 de­ scribes the method. Section 4 presents the study results. Section 5 in­ cludes a discussion of the study findings, and Section 6 is the conclusion.
2. Theoretical background and hypotheses
This section aims to establish the extended TAM model to identify the determining factors of drivers’ acceptance of the AR-HUD system. Based on the foundational TAM, the extended variables from drivers’ characteristics, system service quality, and the human-vehicle interac­ tion were introduced and the corresponding hypotheses are put forward to establish the extended TAM.
2.1. Foundational model
To explain users’ acceptance of new technology, previous studies have developed several theoretical models, such as the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM), Theory of Planned Behavior (TPB), and Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT) [38]. TPB was developed to explain human behavior in general, whereas TAM and UTAUT were specifically developed to explain technology accep­ tance. It was demonstrated that the TAM performs best in analyzing drivers’ acceptance of ADAS [37].
TAM is an adaptation of the theory of reasoned action [39] to model users’ acceptance of information systems, and aims to provide a conceptualized model with a theoretical basis [40]. The foundational TAM consists of four constructs, including two determining factors to accept information technologies, namely, perceived usefulness (PU), perceived ease of use (PEOU), attitude (ATT), and intention to use (IU).

