Simultaneously, additional variables can be extended based on the TAM to improve the explanatory power of the model. Davis et al. (1989) introduced definitions of these factors in the TAM [40]. They defined perceived usefulness as the level to which users believe that a specific technology or service improves overall work performance. Perceived ease of use was defined as the level at which users believe that employing a specific technology or service could be free from mental and physical effort. Attitude was referred to as the users’ preference when they utilize specific technologies or systems. Intention to use was defined as the level of the user’s cognitive state regarding motivation to use the particular services and technologies.
The technology acceptance model holds that system use is deter­ mined by behavioral intention, and behavioral intention is determined by attitude and perceived usefulness. The attitude is determined by the perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use. Perceived usefulness is determined by the perceived ease of use and external variables, and perceived ease of use is determined by external variables. The internal influence relationships among the factors have been confirmed [37,41]. The following hypotheses about the acceptance of AR-HUD were pro­ posed based on foundational TAM:
H1: Perceived ease of use has a significant positive effect on attitude; H2: Perceived ease of use has a significant positive effect on perceived usefulness; H3: Perceived usefulness has a significant positive effect on attitude; H4: Perceived usefulness has a significant positive effect on intention to use; H5: Attitude has a significant positive effect on intention to use.
2.2. Extended variables
In terms of system characteristics, the human–computer interaction process, and personal attributes, we developed five external character­ istics, including trust, information service quality, display service quality, perceived distraction, and driving skills. We hypothesized that these characteristics are likely to contribute significantly to user per­ spectives and the adoption of AR-HUD systems. Specifically,
2.2.1. Trust (TR) Trust was defined as the “attitude that an agent will achieve an in­
dividual’s goals in a situation characterized by uncertainty and vulnerability” [42]. Here, trust is described as the degree to which the driver believes in and relies on AR-HUD in connected environments to achieve the goal of safe driving. Only drivers who trust the system are willing to use AR-HUD and follow its recommendations. Previous studies have shown that trust is a prerequisite of social behavior, especially regarding important decisions. It has been demonstrated that trust is an important factor in influencing consumers’ attitudes and intentions in the context of E-commerce involved in inquiring about and purchasing books on the Internet [43,44], and it is also an important determinant for acceptance of safety–critical systems [45]. It was found that trust is the fourth most important reason for the difference in awareness and willingness of aging drivers to use ADAS [25]. Furthermore, some re­ searchers have confirmed the influence of trust when it is considered as an indirect influence through perceived usefulness [46]. In addition, a positive effect between drivers’ trust and intention to use AVs has been demonstrated [47]. Therefore, the following hypotheses are proposed:
H6(a): Trust has a positive effect on perceived usefulness. H6(b): Trust has a positive effect on attitude. H6(c): Trust has a positive effect on intention to use.
2.2.2. Information service quality (ISQ) & display service quality (DSQ) The definition of “service quality” was first introduced by DeLone
and McLean. They defined it as the degree of the overall performance of an information service and system [48]. Considering that the HUD is a

