special display device, the information service qualityand interface service quality are discussed separately. This paper defines information service quality as the integrity, timeliness, and accuracy of information. Display service quality refers to the artistry and rationality of the interface layout and the effectiveness of information expression. Park and Park (2019) indicated that the functional requirements and inter­ face design and their interplay play an essential role in utility, usability, and usefulness [49]. HUD functions determine the system information contexts and directly affect the system utility perceived by drivers. At the same time, HUD interfaces determine the usability (same definition as the PEOU in the paper) of the system. In addition, Ref. [36] mentioned that the consistency of information presentation and driver expectations directly affect the driver’s trust in the system, that is, the information service quality will directly affect the trust. Another study has also shown a direct relationship between perceived service quality and the intention to use [50]. Therefore,
H7(a): Information service quality is positively associated with perceived usefulness. H7(b): Information service quality is positively associated with trust. H7(c): Information service quality is positively associated with intention to use. H8(a): Display service quality is positively associated with perceived ease of use.
2.2.3. Perceived distraction (PD) Pettitt et al.(2009) proposed a comprehensive definition for driver
distraction, comprising of four components: agent, type, mechanism, and impact. The distraction was described as follows: Due to the agents of some event, activity, object, or person, within or outside the vehicle, the driver’s auditory, biomechanical, cognitive, or visual faculties or combinations of these types were compromised. Following the mecha­ nism of inducing the driver’s shifting attention away from fundamental driving tasks, the driver was impacted and is delayed in the recognition of information necessary to safely maintain the lateral and longitudinal control of the vehicle or the driving task [51]. A HUD could direct gaze toward the road, but overlaying the windshield can also degrade driving performance [52], cause a loss of global situation awareness [53], and cause inattentional blindness [54]. Some studies reported potential safety concerns associated with the use of HUDs, such as visual clutter and cognitive capture [55–57]. With more functionality, more and more information will be generated and make the driving environment very complicated, which makes it very difficult for drivers to digest and respond to all of the information [58]. Displaying too much information through HUDs can result in information overload. Therefore, perceived distraction is defined as the interference of information interaction on the driver’s visual, auditory, and perceptual behaviors in the process of driving. When such interference is considered to negatively impact the main driving task and driving safety, the usefulness, ease of use, and trust in the system will decrease. Therefore, this paper assumes that perceived usefulness has a negative effect on trust, perceived usefulness, and perceived ease of use.
H9(a): Perceived distraction is negatively associated with perceived usefulness. H9(b): Perceived distraction is negatively associated with perceived ease of use. H9(c): Perceived distraction is negatively associated with trust.
2.2.4. Driving skills (DS) The term “driving skills” is defined as drivers’ subjective evaluation
of their ability to drive safely (e.g., emergency response and fluent car control). How drivers perceive their driving skills affects their safety strategies [59], such as adjusting driving behavior and adopting a driving assistance system. Additionally, participants reported their preference through perceived usability and acceptance, and confirmed

