that the dissociation of performance and preference depends on partic­ ipants’ skills [60]. Individuals have disparate information needs ac­ cording to their driving skills, and poorer driving abilities may be associated with more positive perceptions of the system since in­ dividuals with compromised driving skills have more to gain from the technology [17]. With driving, it may be the case that through experi­ ence, fewer conscious resources are required for controlling the vehicle as driving skill becomes automatic and this frees up resources [61]. That means drivers with good driving skills usually have more remaining resources to complete other tasks in driving. Meanwhile, high levels of driving skills are conducive to the driver’s ability to deal with the in­ formation input around the vehicle integrally [62]. Therefore, it’s inferred those drivers with good driving skills usually have more remaining resources and better information processing capability to interact with AR-HUD, influencing the perceived ease of use of AR-HUD. Thus, we inferred that driving skills would affect drivers’ evaluations of the perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use of the system.
H10(a): Driving skills are associated with perceived usefulness. H10(b): Driving skills are associated with perceived ease of use.
