3. Method
3.1. Questionnaire design
A self-administered questionnaire was designed to collect empirical data. The questionnaire consists of three sections. The first section measures respondents’ information with seven items, namely, gender, age, driving experience, driving frequency, educational background, familiarity with HUD, and user experience.
The second section introduced the AR-HUD in a connected envi­ ronment with a 90-second video presenting the design concept, infor­ mation types, and typical application scenarios. Referring to the classification of commercial HUD information and the definition of different driving tasks in previous studies [49,63], the information types were grouped into four categories: vehicle state, safety, navigation, and communication/infotainment/environment. The vehicle state category consists of speed, cruise control-related information, gear shift-related information, RPM, system messages, fuel-related information, turn signal status, etc. The safety category is composed of a collision warning, road sign notification, lane keeping-related information, night visionrelated warning, etc. The navigation category comprises navigation in­ structions, the remaining distance to destination, compass heading, etc. The fourth information category includes radio, audio player, phone call, and outside temperature. The typical application scenarios included speed, AR navigation, lane enhancement, forward collision

