The third section measured respondents’ perceptions about useful­ ness, ease of use, attitude, intentions to use, trust, display service qual­ ity, information service quality, and driving skills. All items were measured with a five-point Likert-type scale, ranging from “strongly disagree (=1)” to “strongly agree (=5)”. The questionnaire was carefully designed and was controlled at 3–4 items in each variable measurement to avoid the abnormally high value of Cronbach’α in reliability analysis caused by a large number of the items. Combined with the system characteristics, different dimensions were also designed to avoid similar questions in variable measurement. Next, the experts’ argument and small sample test were conducted, and the initial questionnaire was revised based on the feedback to avoid ambiguity or redundancy in the items. The items in various constructs in the final questionnaire are shown in Table 1.
3.2. Participants and data collection
Since the number of consumers of AR-HUD products is unknown, a convenience sampling technique was used to collect online question­ naires. In this questionnaire, participants provided their demographic information and answered 29 items in 9 structures. We excluded re­ sponses that were obviously inaccurate based on these five aspects to guarantee the quality and validity of the data: (1) Participants must have a driving license and driving experience over the past year. (2) The duration of the video must be longer than 95 s. (3) The answering time on the specific page must be longer than 30 s. (4) The overall answering time must not be<300 s. (5) The answers on consecutive pages are not completely consistent. Participants submitting valid questionnaires are paid 6–8 yuan per person.
Finally, 388 valid questionnaires were collected. The drivers’ infor­ mation is shown in Table 2. The distribution of participants’ gender, age, driving experience, driving frequency, and educational background were shown. In addition, the participants’ familiarity with HUD and user experience were also investigated.
3.3. Data analysis
Data analysis was completed by Amos and SPSS. In the first stage, we assessed the reliability and validity of the measurement model based on internal consistency, convergent validity, and discriminant validity. In the second stage, structural equation modeling (SEM) was used to verify the hypothetical model framework to explore the relationship between variables and the driver’s acceptance of the AR-HUD.

