4. Results
4.1. Measurement model
The measurement model should be assessed before the structural model is examined, including reliability analysis and validity analysis. Reliability refers to the internal consistency and reliability of measure­ ment results. Cronbach’s α is used to validate internal consistency, and 0.7 or higher is recommended. All Cronbach’s α values exceed 0.7, showing good reliability.
Validity refers to the degree to which the measurement model can accurately measure the target, including structural validity, convergent validity, and discriminant validity. To assess the structural validity, confirmatory factor analysis was performed using AMOS 24.0. χ2 test and its fitting index (e.g., Root Mean Square Error of Approximation (RMSEA), Goodness-of-Fit Index (GFI), Normed Fit Index, (NFI), Comparative Fit Index (CFI), and Incremental Fit Index (IFI)) were used to examine the fitting effect. The χ2/df should be in the range of [1–3], the fitting index should be greater than or equal to 0.90, and RMESA should be<0.08. The results show that χ2/df = 1.736, GFI = 0.908, RMSEA = 0.044, NFI = 0.947, CFI = 0.976, IFI = 0.976, and all the values meet the evaluation criteria.
Convergent validity was assessed based on item loadings, composite reliability (CR), and the average variance extracted (AVE). Item loadings are recommended to exceed 0.6 [68]. The composite reliability is rec­ ommended to exceed 0.7. The average variance extracted (AVE) value for each latent variable should exceed 0.5, and the square root of the AVE should be greater than the inter-construct correlations [69]. As shown in Table 3, all item loadings, all AVEs, and the composite reli­ ability exceeded 0.6, 0.5, and 0.7, respectively. These results showed good convergent validity [70].
To assess discriminant validity, we compared the square root of AVE for each factor to its correlations with other factors. As we can see from Table 4, the square root of AVE for each factor is obviously larger than its correlation coefficients with other factors. Thus, the scale has good

discriminant validity [70]. In addition, the correlation values between latent variables were lower than 0.5, so multicollinearity issues were avoided. Thus, the measurement model is proven to be reliable and valid for the study.
4.2. Structural model
The structural model comprises the hypothesized relationship be­ tween latent constructs in the research model. The value of the fitting index is χ2/df = 1.838, GFI = 0.903, RMSEA = 0.047, NFI = 0.943, CFI = 0.973, and IFI = 0.973. Overall, the predictive validity and model fit indices were satisfactory.
Table 5 shows parameter estimates and the hypothesis results of the extended TAM model. The relationship between variables is expressed by path coefficients. The value of the path coefficient can effectively represent the influence degree between different variables. A positive value represents a positive correlation, while a negative value represents a negative correlation. Finally, the standardized path coefficients and significant results between each latent variable are obtained. The results showed that all hypotheses (H1, H2, H3, H4, H5) introduced in the foundational TAM about variables (PEOU, PU, ATT, and IU) are sup­ ported. For extended variables (TR, ISQ, DSQ, PD, and DS), all hy­ potheses except for H7 (a) and H9 (a) are supported. The results are shown in Table 5 and Fig. 3.
5. Discussion
This study examined the factors affecting drivers’ use intentions of AR-HUD products based on the extended TAM. On the one hand, this paper explores the impact of original variables and extended variables on drivers’ acceptance on the basis of verifying the effectiveness and adaptability of the TAM. On the other hand, some suggestions are put forward for the design and promotion of HUD. In addition, the limita­ tions of the study and areas of future research are pointed out.

