Moreover, it mediates the relationship between information service quality and perceived usefulness. The results indicate that only when the information is trusted can the driver feel that it is useful for driving. All the findings proved that trust is a key factor and precondition of drivers’ acceptance and intention to use connected AR-HUD.
Calibrating the trust of highly diverse users to an appropriate level will help distrusting drivers to more comfortably experience the benefits of AR-HUD [75]. Previous studies demonstrated that augmented reality assistance has the potential to increase user acceptance and trust by communicating system decisions, (i.e., transparent system behavior) to the user as the system proves its awareness of its surroundings [45,76,77]. In addition, a comprehensive level of understanding of the technology seemed to have a positive effect on both acceptability and trust of the technology [78].
Information service quality & Display service quality (ISQ&DSQ). The usability is mainly determined by the HUD interfaces resulting from screen-level interface design. Functional requirements analysis, that is, the specification of HUD functions directly affects the system utility perceived by the drivers [49]. The results show that display service quality significantly affects perceived ease of use (H8(a), β = 0.315, p = 0.001), thus affecting the intention to use, which is consistent with the above research [49]. However, a direct effect between information service quality and perceived usefulness was not observed (H7(a), β = 0.029, p = 0.767). Information service quality indirectly affects perceived usefulness through its strong effect on trust (H7(b), β = 0.953, p < 0.001), and has a direct impact on intention to use (H7(c), β = 0.315, p = 0.001). The results help to further understand the roles of functional requirements analysis and interface design mentioned above and their interplay in the design of automotive HUDs. It can be inferred that although HUD boasts rich functions, only when the information is trusted can it be deemed useful by drivers. Then, in addition to expanding the scope of functions, timeliness, effectiveness, accuracy, and consistency with the driver’s expectations of information (i.e., in­ formation service quality) are important factors affecting HUD accep­ tance. Therefore, on the basis of clarifying drivers’ information needs, the service quality of HUD can be comprehensively improved by providing it to drivers at the right time through reasonable interface design.
Perceived distraction (PD). The results show that perceived distraction has a significant negative effect on perceived ease of use (H9 (b), β = − 0.099, p < 0.001) and trust (H9(c), β = − 0.16, p < 0.001) and has no significant effect on perceived usefulness (H9(a), β = 0.035, p = 0.204). Exactly as the negative effect of visual enhancement design on driver’s tracking errors and subjective workloads was found in previous studies [56,57], participants reported that the AR-HUD caused a certain amount of distraction. However, the negative evaluation did not influ­ ence participants’ perception of the usefulness but mainly lies in the ease of use and trust of the system and has a greater negative impact on trust. Previous studies provide evidence that individual preferences such as willingness to readily adopt new technology might predispose drivers to

