Driving skills (DS). Poor driving skills are related to aberrant driving behaviors (i.e., violations and errors), which are dangerous and may cause an accident [59]. The results demonstrated that driving skills have a positive effect on perceived ease of use (10(b), β = 0.078, p = 0.012) and negatively affect perceived usefulness (H10(a), β = -0.081, p = 0.003). One possible explanation for the negative effect is that the technology may challenge the extensive driving experience that they have acquired over the years, even though drivers need to develop new behavioral patterns [80]. Another possible explanation is that some drivers view ADAS as a threat to their self-image as a good driver rather than a benefit that could make their life easier. This attitude might be reflected in an unrealistically optimistic assessment of their driving skills and a lower intention to use ADAS [1]. This finding indicated that drivers with poor driving skills, such as new drivers and aging drivers, are potential users of AR-HUD systems.
5.2. Suggestions for AR-HUD designer
The lack of public awareness and acceptance of AR-HUD hinders the popularization and advantages of the system [78]. Based on the poten­ tial role of various variables affecting drivers’ acceptance of AR-HUD, this paper provides suggestions to improve drivers’ perceived useful­ ness and perceived ease of use of AR-HUD.
In terms of perceived usefulness, the results show that trust directly affects perceived usefulness, and information service quality also affects

perceived usefulness through trust. At the same time, both trust and information service quality have a direct impact on intention to use. Information service quality and trust are the key factors to improve drivers’ perceived usefulness and acceptance. First, in the aspect of in­ formation service, studies have indicated that drivers want personali­ zation and have diverging content preferences [81]. The integration information should increase sensitivity to the dynamic, contextsensitive, and individual-specific nature of the driver information needs [80]. Second, the integration of different functions in the vehicle should work together to avoid negative safety consequences, such as fighting for attention or giving conflicting information [80]. AR-HUD can be utilized to display novel or unconventional information to enhance the driving experience rather than being positioned as an additional complement for conventional displays. Third, to reduce perceived distractions and improve trust, the information design must achieve a reasonable information flow rate without exceeding human cognition capacities and reduce interference with the driver’s visual, auditory, and perceptual behavior [82]. Fourth, previous research indicated that a comprehensive level of understanding of the system seemed to have a positive effect on both acceptance and trust in the technology [78]. Thus, authorities can publicize the safety improvement performance of AR-HUD to enhance the public’s trust.
In terms of the perceived ease of use, the results indicate that it is affected by display service quality and perceived distraction. Therefore, improving the display service quality and reducing the perceived distraction could be effective ways to enhance drivers’ perceived ease of use. As measured in the questionnaire, we should pay attention to the artistry of the interface, interface layout, and information presentation form in the aspect of interface design. (a) For the artistry of the interface,

