some participants commented that the shape dimension and shape of visual cues expect a combination of design aesthetic and careful place­ ment of visual cues to result in improved user preference [22]. (b) For the interface layout, good interface design would to some extent enable the support of a variety of information types without creating safety problems. Skrypchuk et al. (2019) reconceptualized situation awareness (SA), and the framework facilitates the creation of in-vehicle informa­ tion systems that help drivers succeed in multigoal multitasking situa­ tions [83]. Merenda et al. (2018) demonstrated that vehicle- and worldanimated approaches using directional and motion-based cues can support accurate judgments with a less spatial error of localization than either traditional screen-fixed or world-fixed conformal cues anchored in place [84]. Researchers have proposed the provision of “attentive user interfaces”, which are “UIs that are sensitive to the user’s attention” and “structure communication such that the limited resource of attention is allocated optimally across the user’s tasks” [85]. Additionally, both context switching and focal distance switching are important AR user interface design issues, which should be carefully designed to avoid visual fatigue and driving performance reduction [86]. (c) For the in­ formation presentation form, it is suggested that the representation of the information should be designed with a good self-explanatory ability and be easy to understand.
Furthermore, considering the effect of drivers’ skills on perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use, novice, and aging drivers seemed to be potential consumers of AR-HUD, as individuals with compromised driving skills have more to gain from the technology [17].
5.3. Limitations and future research
The present study of AR-HUD was constrained by several limitations. First, to achieve a balance between time consumption and information transmission, the video used to introduce the AR-HUD only shows typical application scenarios. However, there are diverse AR-HUD products with various functions and practical application scenarios. Thus, the AR-HUD in this paper is a representative case used to identify the key factors affecting acceptance, but the ratings of the variables may not be applicable to all types of AR-HUD. Second, as AR-HUD is not widely available, it is likely that only a small portion of the participants have prior experience with the technology. Third, although previous research indicated that participants could imagine the technology based on the provided description and pictures [87], another study revealed that participants exposed to the driving simulator showed a significantly higher intention to use these systems compared to participants in the online survey [37]. Ref. [42] indicated that perceived ease of use be­ comes a significant predictor of intention to use after the participants directly experienced the AVs. In addition, the subjective questionnaire was used to measure the driver’s driving skills, which will deviate from the driver’s real performance. Therefore, field studies or simulator ex­ periments should be considered to further study the influence of user experience on the acceptance of AR-HUD and practical driving skills. Simultaneously, it is worthwhile to consider the influences of individualrelated factors (e.g., age, gender, education background, income) on drivers’ perception and acceptance of AR-HUD systems. Furthermore, research needs to comprehensively consider the personalized and situ­ ational information needs, interface preferences, and driving safety impacts in connected environments to form AR-HUD design guidance.
6. Conclusions
To address the uncertainty of drivers’ acceptance of AR-HUD and its influencing factors, this study investigated drivers’ acceptance of ARHUD by the extended TAM with additional variables (information ser­ vice quality, display service quality, perceived distraction, trust, and driving skills). The reliability and validity of the measurement model are verified, and relationships between the observed variables are measured by the structural equation model.

