The results indicate that (1) drivers’ trust has a positive effect on perceived usefulness, attitude, and intention to use; (2) display service quality positively affects perceived ease of use, while information ser­ vice quality has a positive effect on trust and intention to use; and (3) perceived distraction has a significantly negative effect on perceived ease of use and trust, and the negative effect on perceived usefulness was not significant. Finally, (4) drivers’ subjective driving skills have a positive effect on perceived ease of use and a negative effect on perceived usefulness.
The further analysis attempts to explain the causes of the influence relationship between variables and provides suggestions to enhance the drivers’ perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use of AR-HUD. The information design is required to consider the sensitivity of information needs, information integration mode, and reasonable information flow. The interface design should comprehensively consider the artistry of the interface, interface layout, and information presentation form. At the same time, enhancing the public’s awareness and trust in the AR-HUD system is necessary, especially for potential users such as novices and aging drivers.
The current study offered insight into the uncertainty and influ­ encing factors of drivers’ acceptance of AR-HUD in theory and supported the development of AR-HUD systems, especially in emerging markets. The findings contributed to improving drivers’ acceptance of AR-HUD and giving full play to AR-HUD’s advantages in connected and auton­ omous vehicles.
